 **Parameters:**
-*   $\xi$: Scale parameter for the half-cosine kernel. Typical value: Not given in the sources.
-*   $\psi$: Scalar correction factor. Precomputed via closed-form regression experiments. Value: Not given in the sources.
+*   **$\xi$ (xi):** Scaling parameter for Half-Cosine kernel. $\xi > 0$ [S1].
+*   **$\psi$ (psi):** Scalar correction factor, precomputed via closed-form regression [S1].
+*   **Covariance $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}$:** Defines anisotropic shape [S1].
 
 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Unbounded Functions:** Functions that grow unbounded cause instability and are omitted; only decaying functions are used [S1].
-*   **Integration Cost:** Using non-Gaussian kernels without the correction factor $\psi$ incurs costly integration processes, making them computationally inefficient [S1].
-*   **Projection Approximation:** The shortcut of skipping the third row/column of the 3D covariance matrix is an approximation. While efficient, it may introduce inaccuracies compared to full integration, though the paper claims it preserves computational efficiency [S1].
+*   **Unbounded Functions:** Growing functions cause instability and are omitted; only decaying functions are used [S1].
+*   **Integration Cost:** Using functions outside the Gaussian family typically incurs costly integration. The correction factor $\psi$ is critical to avoid this [S1].
+*   **Finite Support:** Half-Cosine has finite support ($d_M^2 < \frac{\xi \pi}{2}$), which must be handled in rasterization bounds [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Training Speed:** The half-cosine kernel variant achieves 34% faster training convergence compared to standard Gaussian kernels [S1].
-*   **Memory Usage:** The half-cosine kernel reduces memory usage by 15% compared to standard Gaussian kernels [S1].
-*   **Visual Quality:** Achieves comparable PSNR, SSIM, and LPIPS results to standard 3DGS [S1].
-*   **Kernel Spread:** Half-cosine and Inverse MQ functions span a larger region with higher opacity for the same variance, reducing the need for multiple splats to achieve the same accumulated value [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Faster training, lower memory footprint, plug-and-play replacement for Gaussian kernels [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on an approximation for 2D projection; requires precomputed correction factors for optimal performance [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training convergence is 34% faster compared to standard Gaussian kernels [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is reduced by 15% compared to standard Gaussian kernels [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Achieves comparable PSNR, SSIM, and LPIPS results to 3DGS [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** More efficient alternative for repetitive industrial applications where computational savings are critical; wider-spread kernels reduce the need for multiple splats [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires precomputed correction factors; limited to specific DARBF variants (Half-Cosine, Inverse MQ) [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When training time and memory footprint are critical constraints and comparable visual quality to 3DGS is acceptable.
+*   **When not to use:** When the absolute highest fidelity of the exponential family Gaussian is required, or if the overhead of managing correction factors is undesirable.
 
 ## Background
-DARBF-Splatting was introduced by Hashiru Pramuditha, Vinasirajan Viruthshaan, Vishagar Arunan, Saeedha Nazar, Sameera Ramasinghe, Simon Lucey, and Ranga Rodrigo from the University of Moratuwa and University of Adelaide [S1]. It was published in 2026 [S1]. The method addresses the limitation of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) being restricted to the exponential family of functions, generalizing splatting to non-exponential decaying anisotropic radial basis functions [S1].
+DARBF-Splatting was introduced by Hashiru Pramuditha, Vinasirajan Viruthshaan, Vishagar Arunan, Saeedha Nazar, Sameera Ramasinghe, Simon Lucey, and Ranga Rodrigo from the University of Moratuwa and University of Adelaide [S1]. It was published in 2026 in the IEEE Winter Conference on Applications of Computer Vision (WACV) [S1]. The method addresses the underexplored area of non-exponential reconstruction kernels in splatting-based 3D reconstruction, which had been limited by the computational inefficiency of integrating non-Gaussian functions [S1].
 
